# Hej Nathejk — Agent Rules

## Org conventions
Read `.agents/rules/rules.md` for org-wide conventions before proceeding.
Everything in that file applies here unless explicitly overridden below.

---

## This repo

**Purpose:** Nathejk "Hej Nathejk" — in-event companion app (maps, contacts, rulebook, event updates), user-facing frontend with a dedicated api (backend-for-frontend style)
**Group:** Core

### Services

| Service | Technology | Dev target | Local URL |
|---|---|---|---|
| frontend | Vue 3 (TS) | `ui-dev` | `https://hej.local.nathejk.dk` |
| bff | Go | `base` | internal only |

### Three surfaces, two frontends — **do not mix them**

The table above says "frontend: Vue 3", and that is only a third of the picture. This repo serves **three
user-facing surfaces**, and the `bff` is not internal-only: it server-renders two of them as HTML.

| Surface | Who it is for | Where it lives | Stack |
|---|---|---|---|
| **The PWA** | participants, on a phone | `vue/` | Vue 3 + TS, Vite, Tailwind v4, shadcn-vue, Lucide. Installable, service worker, push. |
| **The public website** | families and the open web | `go/cmd/api/publicsite.go` | Go `html/template`, hand-written CSS inline in the template, vendored Leaflet. **No build step.** |
| **The admin tool** | 2–3 photographers/curators, on a laptop | `go/cmd/api/adminui/` | Go `html/template` + **Pico + htmx + Alpine**, all vendored. Real `.html`/`.css`/`.js` files. **No build step.** |

**This separation is a hard rule, not a preference** (maintainer, 2026-09-23):

> *"It's essential that we do not mix the pwa and the website, it's two different things and should be kept as
> such."*

Why it holds, so it is not re-litigated:

- **The PWA is install-only and phone-only.** `vue/src/router/gates.ts` runs a device-class gate **ahead of**
  the auth gate and `window.location.replace`s a desktop visitor out to the public website. Bulk upload from an
  SD card is desktop work by definition, so the admin tool *cannot* live in the PWA — see PRD 022 §8.1.
- **The website surfaces have no build step, and that is the point.** They must render from the Go binary
  alone. This is why **Tailwind is not available to them**: Tailwind's mechanism is scanning markup at build
  time, the only Tailwind here lives inside the PWA's Vite build, and using it on the website would mean either
  a second pipeline or coupling the two things that must stay apart. Pico was chosen for the admin tool for
  exactly that reason (PRD 022 §7, task 395).
- **They have different privacy postures.** The PWA is authenticated; the public website names no person
  (PRD 011 §0b.1) with one narrow documented exception, a photographer's credit line (task 393).

**Practical consequences when picking up work here:**

- Working in `vue/`? Use the `vue3-pwa-layout` skill. shadcn-vue, Tailwind, Lucide components all apply.
- Working on a page the Go service renders — anything under `/{year}/…` or `/admin` — use the
  `go-server-rendered-pages` skill. **shadcn-vue and Tailwind do not apply and are not available.**
- Do not add an `npm` dependency, a bundler or a build step to the website surfaces. Third-party libraries are
  **vendored, pinned and served from the Go binary** (`go/cmd/api/adminui/vendor/`), never from a CDN — a CDN
  would let a third party log who looked at the event's photographs.
- Do not put website assets under `vue/`. (The website's map island and its Leaflet copy currently *are* served
  from `vue/public/` — a pre-existing wrinkle worth untangling, not a precedent to follow. New website assets
  are embedded in the Go binary; see `go/cmd/api/adminui/`.)

### Key files
- `docker/Dockerfile` — multistage build (dev targets: `ui-dev`, `base`; prod target: `prod`)
- `docker-compose.yml` — dev environment
- `docker-compose.prod.yml` — production stack (plain compose; the org does **not**
  use Swarm — see task 089)
- `roadmap/tasks/TASKS.md` — task board conventions
- `roadmap/prd/` — Product Requirements Documents (see `roadmap/prd/README.md`)

### Frontend skills

There are two, and which one applies is decided by **where the file lives**, not by what you are building:

- **`vue3-pwa-layout`** — anything in `vue/`. The participants' installable PWA.
- **`go-server-rendered-pages`** — anything the Go service renders as HTML: the public website
  (`go/cmd/api/publicsite.go`) and the admin tool (`go/cmd/api/adminui/`).

The older `vue3-spa-layout-legacy` skill describes the PrimeVue-based sibling repos and
must **not** be applied here.

---

## Repo-specific conventions

- All endpoints must have OpenAPI annotations
- New features and significant changes require a PRD (`prd` skill) before implementation
- **Guardian phone numbers never enter the PWA**, with exactly one exception: a user
  confirming or approving **their own** guardian's number (onboarding / profile —
  PRD 003, PRD 005). No other surface may carry a `phoneParent`: not a directory, not
  a contact list, not a lookup, not a cached manifest. This is a hard rule rather than
  a per-feature judgement, because a guardian's number is the most sensitive field in
  the record and the person it belongs to is not a user of this app and never agreed
  to be in it. If an operational need for one appears, it belongs in an organizer tool
  behind its own decision — not in the participants' app. BFF responses must project
  the field out rather than relying on the client not to render it.
- **Component library (the PWA only):** use [shadcn-vue](https://www.shadcn-vue.com/) for all UI
  components in `vue/` (see the `vue3-pwa-layout` skill). **Prefer a standard shadcn-vue
  component whenever one exists** — only hand-roll a component if nothing in the
  catalogue fits, and note why in a comment. Generated primitives live in
  `vue/src/components/ui/` and are owned source — edit them in place rather than
  wrapping them to patch styling.
  **Do not use PrimeVue.** It is being removed (see PRD 004) and must not be
  reintroduced: no `primevue`, no `@primevue/*`, no Lara/theme presets, no
  PrimeIcons.
  **This does not reach the website surfaces.** The public site and the admin tool are server-rendered Go
  templates with no build step, so there is no shadcn-vue there and no way to add it — see "Three surfaces"
  above and the `go-server-rendered-pages` skill.
- **Styling (the PWA only):** Tailwind **v4**, configured CSS-first in `vue/src/assets/main.css`
  (`@import "tailwindcss"`, `@theme`). Do not reintroduce `tailwind.config.js`.
  **Tailwind is not available to the website surfaces** and must not be introduced to them: it needs a build
  step, and the only pipeline here belongs to the PWA. The admin tool uses vendored **Pico**; the public site
  uses hand-written CSS in its template.
- **Typography:** all **major headlines use the Nathejk font** — in the PWA via the
  `font-nathejk` utility, backed by `--font-nathejk` (Impact + narrow-bold
  fallbacks) in `vue/src/assets/main.css`. That covers page `<h1>`s, title-level
  section headings and the app bar wordmark; body text and controls stay on the
  system sans stack. Never set a font family in a Vue component.
  On the **server-rendered** surfaces the same intent is met with a plain font stack in the page's own CSS,
  because there is no utility layer to reach for — declared once per stylesheet, not per rule.
- **Icons:** use [Lucide](https://lucide.dev/) for all icons across the project.
  In the PWA that is the `@lucide/vue` components; on the **server-rendered** surfaces it is
  **inline SVG copied from Lucide**, because there is no build step to import a component through — same icon
  set, different delivery. Do not introduce other icon sets (e.g. PrimeIcons, Font Awesome) for UI icons.
- **Browser baseline:** **iOS/iPadOS Safari 16.4+** and **Chrome 111+** (Android
  equivalent). This is not a constraint we chose for tooling's sake — it is what
  the product already requires: iOS only supports **Web Push for home-screen web
  apps from 16.4**, and the app's core features (service worker, push,
  geolocation, installability) need a modern engine and a secure context anyway.
  Tailwind v4's requirement (Safari 16.4+ / Chrome 111+) lands on exactly the same
  floor, so it costs us no reach. Don't add polyfills or fallbacks for older
  engines; those users cannot use the app's primary features regardless.
